00:00:32.878 --> 00:00:36.964
An ambiguously long time ago, there was this
theory of medicine.

00:00:36.964 --> 00:00:41.056
It was called the Doctrine of Signatures,
and it persisted for centuries,

00:00:41.056 --> 00:00:42.789
maybe even millennia.

00:00:42.789 --> 00:00:47.629
The liverworts we’re looking at right now
are a prime example of how this system conceptualized

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:50.010
nature and medicine.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.795
The premise of the Doctrine of Signatures
was so simple that honestly,

00:00:53.795 --> 00:00:55.072
we wish it were true.

00:00:55.072 --> 00:00:59.540
The idea was that if you came across a plant
that looked like a body part, that meant it

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.868
was meant to treat ailments that targeted
said part.

00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:08.369
In the 17th century, an English physician
named Nicholas Culpepper said this of liverwort:

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:14.690
It is a singular good Herb for all the diseases
of the Liver, both to cool and cleanse it.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:22.698
All this pressure put on one tiny plant, simply
because it resembled the liver.

00:01:22.698 --> 00:01:26.442
Unfortunately, it turns out that medicine
is a little more complicated than the

00:01:26.442 --> 00:01:28.087
Doctrine of Signatures.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:32.799
But even though liverworts cannot cure all
the diseases of the liver, it’s still remarkably

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:37.740
talented, capable of cloning itself in a series
of steps that sound like they came right out

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.650
of science fiction.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:43.351
To get a sense of what liverworts look like
in nature, we can actually turn back to

00:01:43.351 --> 00:01:45.519
Nicholas Culpepper’s writing:

00:01:45.519 --> 00:01:51.100
THe Common Liverwort, groweth close and spreadeth
much upon the ground in moyst and shadowy

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:57.470
places, with many sad green leaves, or rather
(as it were) sticking flat one to another,

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:03.549
very unevenly cut in on the edges, and crumpled,
from among which arise smal slender stalks

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:08.170
an Inch or two high at most, bearing smal
Starlike Flowers at the tops:

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.076
The Roots are very fine and smal.

00:02:12.669 --> 00:02:16.880
The flowery structures and roots that Culpepper
described might make you think that liverworts

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.670
are just a normal plant.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.530
But liverworts are bryophytes—

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.290
a loosely associated group of ancient but
tiny plants that don’t have the vasculature

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.449
we think of when we think of flowering plants.

00:02:28.449 --> 00:02:33.480
Without vascular structures like a xylem or
phloem to transport nutrients and water around,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.764
bryophytes can’t grow quite as tall.

00:02:35.764 --> 00:02:40.459
Instead, liverworts and their bryophyte compatriots,
including mosses, tend to grow only a few

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.000
centimeters tall.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.210
Even though they’re super low to the ground,
liverworts make quite an impression.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:51.305
For one, they're bright green, filled with
chlorophyll to help them perform photosynthesis.

00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:55.560
But if you scan along the surface of some
liverwort species, you might notice weird

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.569
parts where the cells seem to warp and turn
in a strange way, their insides filled with

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:03.242
little discs made out of plant tissue.

00:03:03.242 --> 00:03:04.027
Why?

00:03:04.027 --> 00:03:05.633
We’ll get back to that in a bit.

00:03:07.826 --> 00:03:09.420
There are two types of liverworts.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.620
The less common kind are called leafy liverworts
because of their leafy scales and flat stems.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.480
These liverworts look a lot like moss, but
their leafy structures are distinct enough

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.809
that those who know what to look 
for can distinguish the two.

00:03:21.809 --> 00:03:26.310
The other kind of liverworts are thalloid
liverworts, which look more like scaly structures

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.218
stretching out across the ground.

00:03:28.218 --> 00:03:33.271
When Culpepper described liverworts as having
small, fine roots, he was almost correct.

00:03:33.271 --> 00:03:37.641
Liverworts do have small, hairy structures
that help it latch onto surfaces.

00:03:37.641 --> 00:03:42.120
These structures are called rhizoids, unlike
roots, they aren’t able to absorb nutrients

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.879
from their surroundings.

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:48.379
These rhizoids are fascinating to look at
because each one is a single cell, stretched

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:51.744
out long and able to hold the liverwort in
place.

00:03:51.744 --> 00:03:55.980
Another feature that distinguishes bryophytes
like the liverwort from vascular plants is

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.148
that they don’t reproduce with seeds.

00:03:58.148 --> 00:04:01.989
Instead they rely on spores for sexual reproduction.

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:06.360
To make this happen, liverworts have two distinct
stages to their life.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:11.690
They begin as a gametophyte, with sperm-producing
structures called antheridia and egg-bearing

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.060
structures called archegonia.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:19.400
The antheridia produce sperm, which travels
through water to find the archegonia on another

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.831
liverwort so that it can fertilize the eggs
there.

00:04:22.831 --> 00:04:27.710
And when that fertilization happens, a new
stage of liverwort emerges into the world

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.675
called a sporophyte.

00:04:29.675 --> 00:04:33.870
It will continue developing in the archegonia
until it’s ready to release spores into

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.416
the world, which will develop into a 
new gametophyte, carrying on the cycle.

00:04:38.416 --> 00:04:40.816
That process is so sweet and familiar:

00:04:40.816 --> 00:04:45.759
an egg and a sperm meet, and from there an
entity develops into a life, expanding the

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:48.698
world of liverworts just a little bit further.

00:04:48.698 --> 00:04:54.118
But thalloid liverworts are capable of reproducing
by another means, a weirder one.

00:04:54.118 --> 00:04:57.309
All they need is a cup and some rain.

00:04:57.309 --> 00:04:59.498
Remember that green swirl we saw earlier?

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:03.647
That structure is on the surface of the liverwort,
and it’s called the gemma cup.

00:05:03.647 --> 00:05:08.129
Its job is to hold little discs of liverwort
tissue called gemmae.

00:05:08.129 --> 00:05:12.449
The gemma cup and its little gemmae form during
the gametophyte stage, but they don’t do

00:05:12.449 --> 00:05:13.867
anything at this point.

00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:16.207
The gemmae are simply dormant.

00:05:16.207 --> 00:05:19.181
So what does it take to awaken the gemmae?

00:05:19.181 --> 00:05:20.048
Rain.

00:05:20.048 --> 00:05:21.418
Seriously, that’s it.

00:05:21.418 --> 00:05:26.741
When rain pours onto the liverwort, it splashes
the gemmae out of its cup and carries them away.

00:05:26.741 --> 00:05:30.720
And as they travel, the gemmae emerge from
their slumber so that eventually, when they

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.525
find a place to land, they become a new plant.

00:05:33.525 --> 00:05:35.754
Though maybe “new” is the wrong way to
put it.

00:05:35.754 --> 00:05:39.850
They’re actually a clone of the old one,
born from just a little disc carried in a

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:42.871
cup and pushed out with just a dash of rain.

00:05:42.871 --> 00:05:48.030
We have seen so many versions of asexual reproduction
in the microcosmos, and they often feel kind

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.030
of violent—

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.979
or at least painful, if microbes felt the
pain of tearing themselves apart into two

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:54.760
identical clones.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:59.100
In comparison, the liverwort’s method feels
so sweet, like it’s just packing its little

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.448
future selves for a rainy journey.

00:06:01.448 --> 00:06:05.139
It’s a version of clones and asexual reproduction
that we probably wouldn’t have conjured

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:08.144
up in our wildest science fiction imaginations.

00:06:08.144 --> 00:06:09.150
It’s adorable.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:13.699
A little bit of rain falls in a cup, and out
comes a sleepy clone, ready to wake up and

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:18.628
spread its wings, or, I suppose, its non-vascular
body, into the world.

